// core:fmt — where rendered bytes go, and what shape they arrive in.
//
//     import "core:fmt"
//
//     [64]char8 store
//     fmt.Cursor c = fmt.over(store[:])
//     Sink out = fmt.to_bytes(&c)
//
//     _ = fmt.put_string("n = ", out)
//     _ = fmt.put_signed(42, out)
//     string line = fmt.written(c)       // the bytes, as a string
//
// **The compiler owns what a value looks like; this package owns where it goes
// and how it is shaped.** `#render(v, sink)` renders one value in the one form
// its type has — no width, no precision, no base, no alignment, because a
// renderer driven by a type cannot take an opinion from a call. Every one of
// those opinions lives here, and each is an ordinary function a program can
// read.
//
// ## The four that write a message
//
//     public void print(..#any args)
//     public void println(..#any args)
//     public void write(Sink out, ..#any args)
//     public void writeln(Sink out, ..#any args)
//
// These are the compiler's retired `print` and `println`, and they are this
// package's because the destination is a library's business and the *rendering*
// is not: `#render(v, sink)` is the compiler's, per-type, and these four are a
// `..#any` pack unrolled over it. They exist only inside a `when #OS != #NONE:`
// — on a freestanding target the names do not exist and a call is refused
// rather than silently doing nothing. `print` and `println` reach the terminal
// through `core:io`'s `stdout_sink()`; `write` and `writeln` take the sink the
// caller names. None of them fails, so no call site writes `try`.
//
// They are the one place the sink is written **first**, and the rule below says
// why that is the grammar's doing rather than a choice.
//
// ## The sink is last, and it is not optional
//
// Every function below takes its `Sink` as the final parameter, because that is
// where the language already puts the argument that says *where this goes*:
// `#format(v…, alloc)` writes the allocator last and `#render(v…, sink)` writes
// the sink last, and both for the same reason — a value has a rendering and a
// destination does not, so the position is unambiguous by type and a reader
// never counts arguments to find it.
//
// A function that renders and hides its destination is the shape this package
// exists to not have.
//
// ## Everything answers `i64`, and it is the count the sink took
//
// Not `void` and not `bool`. A sink that took *some* of the bytes is a real
// state — `to_bytes` over a full buffer is exactly that — so the answer is a
// count, and a caller that cares compares it against what it asked for. It is
// `#render`'s own answer and the two compose: a putter's result is the number a
// `#render` into the same sink would have added.
//
// A nil `proc` takes nothing and answers 0, so an unset `Sink` is inert rather
// than a fault.
//
// ## This package includes nothing, and imports one thing
//
// No `foreign` block — so this file emits no `#include` of its own, and a
// program on a target whose OS is `#NONE` may use every line of it that exists
// there. That is not an accident of what happened to be needed: it is the
// reason the stream sinks are **not here**. `stdout` is `<stdio.h>`, a
// `foreign` block's `#include` reaches every importer of the package holding
// it, and a `fmt` that dragged `<stdio.h>` behind it would be a formatting
// library no freestanding program could call. `core:io` owns `^FILE` already
// and is where a sink over one belongs.
//
// The one `import` is `core:io`, and it is what the four printing functions at
// the foot of this file reach standard output through. It costs an importer
// nothing it does not ask for, because the emitter follows **liveness** and not
// imports: those four are templates over a pack, so a program that calls none
// of them instantiates none of them and emits no `<stdio.h>`. The argument for
// the rule above is therefore unchanged — what it forbids is a header this
// package *cannot* avoid emitting, and an import is not one.
//
// The same rule keeps `core:math` out: `<math.h>` and `-lm` would ride along
// with it — unconditionally, because the float path below is concrete and calls
// it — so that path does its own arithmetic.
//
// ## What an importer pays, measured
//
// **Everything concrete in a package is emitted whether it is called or not**,
// and the emitter's one exemption is an uncalled *template*. That is the
// budget this package is written against, and the sharpened form of the rule is
// worth stating because it is the half that is not obvious:
//
// > A template is free only when nothing **concrete in its own package** calls
// > it. A concrete function is always emitted, so every template it reaches is
// > instantiated along with it, into every importer.
//
// Measured on this package, with a program calling three of its functions:
// 954 lines of emitted C and 35 definitions before that rule was applied, and
// **805 lines and 29 definitions after** — the difference being `put_quoted`
// reaching the public `put_hex` template and the float path reaching the public
// `put_unsigned` and `put_signed_pad` ones. Every internal call now goes to a
// private primitive (`render_base`, `digit`, `write`), so the public templates
// are instantiated by callers and by nothing else.
//
// The float path is 129 of the remaining 568 lines of function bodies — 22% —
// and it is **not** split into a package of its own. The split was priced: it
// would have to make `render_base` and `digit` public to serve it, growing the
// public surface in order to shrink the emitted C, and the `strings/parse`
// precedent it would be citing is about a **capability** difference (a header
// decides which targets may import the package) rather than a size one. The
// numbers are here rather than the split, so a program that finds 129 lines
// intolerable has them to argue with.
//
// ## What is deliberately absent
//
// **There is no format string.** A literal is never a format string anywhere in
// this language — the format is written by the compiler from each argument's
// type — and a library that introduced one would be re-opening that decision
// with a second, weaker parser. What replaces it is a `Pad` value and a putter
// per shape: the opinion is a *value* you can name, hoist and reuse, and it is
// checked by the type system rather than by a runtime scan of a string.
package fmt

import "core:io"

// ── the bytes, and reading raw ones ──────────────────────────────────────────
//
// A sink's procedure is handed a `rawptr` and a count, which is C's shape and
// not Dado's. `Raw` is the one name that turns it back into something with a
// length: a `^char8` is what a `rawptr` converts to for free, and a window taken
// over it is a `[]char8` of the length the caller stated. Same idiom, same
// reason, as `core:mem`'s `Bytes`.
private type Raw: ^char8

// The widest thing `put_base` can produce: 64 binary digits of a `u64`, plus a
// sign, plus a two-character radix prefix, plus slack. Every scratch buffer in
// this file is this size so there is one number to reason about.
private const i32 SCRATCH = 80

private type Scratch: [SCRATCH]char8

// ── writing ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// Hand `bytes` to `out`, and answer how many it took.
//
// **This is the only place in the package that calls a sink**, which is what
// makes the nil guard and the zero-length guard true of every function below
// rather than of the ones that remembered. The `rawptr(&bytes[0])` is the
// conversion at the boundary and it is guarded, because a zero-length slice has
// no element 0 whose address could be taken.
//
// **Named `put_bytes` and not `write`**, which is the name it had until the
// printing verbs arrived. `write(Sink, ..#any)` needs the shorter word: the
// four printing functions are a family, `write` and `writeln` are two of them,
// and a package cannot spell one name twice. The rename also puts this
// function where a reader looks for it — beside `put_string` and `put_char`,
// which is what it is, one putter among the putters.
i64 put_bytes([]char8 bytes, Sink out):
    i64 count = #len(bytes)
    if count <= 0:
        return 0

    if out.proc == nil:
        return 0

    return out.proc(out.data, rawptr(&bytes[0]), count)

// The bytes of a `string`, written whole — embedded `\0` included, because a
// `string` is its length and has no opinion about its contents.
//
// `rawptr(s)` reads the pointer slot, which is one of the conversions the
// language admits, so this costs no window and no walk: a `string` is already
// the pointer and the count this needs.
i64 put_string(string8 s, Sink out):
    i64 count = #len(s)
    if count <= 0:
        return 0

    if out.proc == nil:
        return 0

    return out.proc(out.data, rawptr(s), count)

// The bytes at `c`, up to its `\0`. One walk to find the length C threw away —
// `string(c)` is the borrowing conversion and `nil` borrows as the empty string,
// so a `cstring` nobody checked writes nothing rather than faulting.
i64 put_cstring(cstring c, Sink out):
    return put_string(string8(c), out)

// One byte.
i64 put_char(char8 b, Sink out):
    Scratch one
    one[0] = b
    return put_bytes(one[0..<1], out)

// `b`, `n` times. `n <= 0` writes nothing. This is what every alignment in the
// package is built on, and it is public because a caller drawing a rule or an
// indent wants it directly.
i64 put_repeat(char8 b, i32 n, Sink out):
    if n <= 0:
        return 0

    Scratch fill
    i32 chunk = n
    if chunk > SCRATCH:
        chunk = SCRATCH

    i32 zero = 0
    for i in zero..<chunk:
        fill[i] = b

    i64 wrote = 0
    i32 left = n
    for left > 0:
        i32 take = left
        if take > SCRATCH:
            take = SCRATCH

        wrote = wrote + put_bytes(fill[0..<i64(take)], out)
        left = left - take

    return wrote

// ── alignment ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// Which side the padding goes on. A plain `i32`-backed enum, so it orders and
// compares with nothing written.
enum i32 Align:
    Left,
    Right,
    Center,

// How a field is padded: how wide it is at least, which side the slack goes on,
// and what the slack is made of. A `width` of 0 or less pads nothing, which is
// what makes `PLAIN` below the do-nothing value rather than a special case
// every putter has to test for.
//
// It is a value rather than a set of parameters on purpose: a program that
// formats a table names its column once and passes the name, where a parameter
// list would have it repeat three arguments at every call and get one of them
// wrong eventually.
type Pad:
    i32   width,
    Align align,
    char8 fill,

// No padding. The value an argument takes when a caller does not care.
const Pad PLAIN = (0, Align.Left, ' ')

// Right-aligned in `width` columns, space-filled — a number in a column.
Pad right(i32 width):
    return (width, Align.Right, ' ')

// Left-aligned in `width` columns, space-filled — a name in a column.
Pad left(i32 width):
    return (width, Align.Left, ' ')

// Right-aligned in `width` columns, zero-filled — a fixed-width number.
//
// **The zero goes where the space would**, which for a negative number puts it
// between the sign and the digits only because the sign is part of what is being
// padded. `put_signed_pad(-7, zeros(4), out)` is `0-07` and not `-007`. That is the
// honest consequence of padding being a property of the *field* rather than of
// the number, and a caller who wants the other one writes the sign itself.
Pad zeros(i32 width):
    return (width, Align.Right, '0')

// The slack before the body, and the slack after it. `n` is how many bytes the
// body will write, which every caller below already knows without rendering it
// twice — that is the whole reason these are separate functions rather than one
// wrapper taking a putter.
private (i32 before, i32 after) slack(i32 n, Pad p):
    if p.width <= n:
        return (0, 0)

    i32 total = p.width - n
    switch p.align:
        case Align.Left:
            return (0, total)
        case Align.Right:
            return (total, 0)
        case Align.Center:
            i32 head = total / 2
            return (head, total - head)
        else:
            return (0, total)

// ── integers ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// One digit of `base`, as the character it is written with.
private char8 digit(u32 d, bool upper):
    if d < 10:
        return char8(i32('0') + i32(d))

    if upper:
        return char8(i32('A') + i32(d) - 10)

    return char8(i32('a') + i32(d) - 10)

// `value` in `base`, into `tmp`, answering how many characters it wrote.
//
// The digits come out least-significant first and are reversed in place, which
// is one pass and one buffer rather than a division count and a second walk.
// A base outside 2..=36 has no digits to write with and produces nothing, which
// the two public callers turn into a written 0.
private i32 render_base(^Scratch tmp, u64 value, u32 base, bool upper):
    if base < 2 || base > 36:
        return 0

    i32 n = 0
    u64 v = value
    if v == 0:
        tmp^[0] = '0'
        return 1

    for v > 0:
        u64 d = v % u64(base)
        tmp^[n] = digit(u32(d), upper)
        n = n + 1
        v = v / u64(base)

    i32 lo = 0
    i32 hi = n - 1
    for lo < hi:
        char8 swap = tmp^[lo]
        tmp^[lo] = tmp^[hi]
        tmp^[hi] = swap
        lo = lo + 1
        hi = hi - 1

    return n

// An unsigned value in `base`, with the digits above 9 written in the case
// asked for. `base` outside 2..=36 writes a single `0` rather than nothing, so
// the function is total and a caller never has to test a base it computed.
i64 put_base(u64 value, u32 base, bool upper, Sink out):
    Scratch tmp
    i32 n = render_base(&tmp, value, base, upper)
    if n == 0:
        return put_char('0', out)

    return put_bytes(tmp[0..<i64(n)], out)

// ── the decimal putters, and why they are templates ─────────────────────────
//
// **These bind `$T` rather than taking an `i64`, for two reasons and the second
// is the one that decided it.**
//
// The first is the call site: a program holding an `i32` would otherwise write
// `put_i64(i64(n), out)` at every call, and a library that makes its caller
// write a widening conversion to print a number is a library with a `printf`
// problem in a different shape.
//
// The second is what an importer pays. The emitter writes **every** function of
// every live package, and its one exemption is an uncalled *template* — so a
// concrete `put_i64` is in the emitted C of a program that never prints an
// integer, and this is not. Measured on this package before the change: a
// program calling three of these functions emitted all thirty-five of them and
// 954 lines of C against a 12-line baseline. Every function here that can
// honestly bind a `$` now does, and what a program does not call it does not
// carry.
//
// **The signed and unsigned halves cannot be one template**, and the reason is
// worth knowing because it will come up again. The `int` category admits all
// eight widths, and the signed body has to ask `v < 0` — which is always false
// for an unsigned `T` and is a diagnostic under the `-Wextra -Werror` the
// emitted C is built with. So one template over `int` would compile for four
// widths and fail the build for the other four. The domain is stated instead,
// in the one place a domain belongs: a `where` clause, asked at the call, which
// answers a `put_signed(u32)` with ERR0919 naming the call rather than with a C
// warning naming a line the caller never wrote.
//
// **The `where` says `T is int` first, and not only the list, and that
// difference is the API.** A whole-type binding is inferred only from an
// argument that has a type, so `put_signed(42, out)` under a bare `$T` is
// ERR0504 — an untyped literal supplies no answer of its own. A `where` that
// pins a category places the literal in the category's default, so the
// literal binds `i32` and the call reads the way it should; a list alone
// would place it in its first member, `i8`. The one place the wart survives is a negated literal: `0 - 42` is two
// untyped literals and still says nothing, so write `i32(0 - 42)` or bind it
// first.

// A signed integer of any width, in decimal.
//
// **The negation is done in `u64`.** The most negative value of a width has no
// positive counterpart, so negating in the signed width is the one input where
// the obvious code wraps to itself and prints the wrong number. Widening to
// `u64` first — which sign-extends, so `-5` becomes `0xFFFF…FB` — and
// subtracting that from zero is exact for every input including that one.
i64 put_signed($T value, Sink out) where T is int && T in (i8, i16, i32, i64):
    if value >= 0:
        return put_base(u64(value), 10, false, out)

    u64 magnitude = 0 - u64(value)
    i64 wrote = put_char('-', out)
    return wrote + put_base(magnitude, 10, false, out)

// An unsigned integer of any width, in decimal.
i64 put_unsigned($T value, Sink out) where T is int && T in (u8, u16, u32, u64):
    return put_base(u64(value), 10, false, out)

// `value` in decimal, as `put_signed` writes it, at the start of `into`.
// Answers how many bytes it wrote, or 0 and writes nothing when they do not
// fit; 20 bytes always fit. It takes no `Sink`, so a script can call it.
//
//     [20]char8 buf
//     i32 n = fmt.render_signed(i64(-42), buf[:])    // 3: buf starts "-42"
#assert_raises
i32 render_signed($T value, []char8 into) where T is int && T in (i8, i16, i32, i64):
    Scratch digits
    if value >= 0:
        return copy_into(&digits, 0, render_base(&digits, u64(value), 10, false), into)

    digits[0] = '-'
    Scratch rest
    i32 n = render_base(&rest, 0 - u64(value), 10, false)
    for i in 0..<n:
        digits[i + 1] = rest[i]

    return copy_into(&digits, 0, n + 1, into)

// `value` in decimal, as `put_unsigned` writes it, at the start of `into`.
// Answers how many bytes it wrote, or 0 and writes nothing when they do not
// fit; 20 bytes always fit. It takes no `Sink`, so a script can call it.
//
//     [20]char8 buf
//     i32 n = fmt.render_unsigned(u64(600), buf[:])    // 3: buf starts "600"
#assert_raises
i32 render_unsigned($T value, []char8 into) where T is int && T in (u8, u16, u32, u64):
    Scratch digits
    return copy_into(&digits, 0, render_base(&digits, u64(value), 10, false), into)

// `text[from..<from + n]` copied to the start of `into`, answering `n`; or 0,
// copying nothing, when `into` is shorter than `n`.
private i32 copy_into(^Scratch text, i32 from, i32 n, []char8 into):
    if i64(n) > #len(into):
        return 0

    for i in 0..<n:
        into[i] = text^[from + i]

    return n

// Hexadecimal, with an optional `0x` and an optional minimum digit count that
// is met with leading zeroes. `digits` is a floor and never a ceiling — a value
// too wide for it is written whole, because a number silently truncated to fit
// a field is the one failure mode a formatter must not have.
i64 put_hex($T value, i32 digits, bool upper, bool prefix, Sink out) where T is int && T in (u8, u16, u32, u64):
    Scratch tmp
    i32 n = render_base(&tmp, u64(value), 16, upper)
    i64 wrote = 0
    if prefix:
        wrote = wrote + put_char('0', out)
        if upper:
            wrote = wrote + put_char('X', out)
        else:
            wrote = wrote + put_char('x', out)

    if digits > n:
        wrote = wrote + put_repeat('0', digits - n, out)

    return wrote + put_bytes(tmp[0..<i64(n)], out)

// Binary, with an optional `0b` and the same floor rule as `put_hex`.
i64 put_binary($T value, i32 digits, bool prefix, Sink out) where T is int && T in (u8, u16, u32, u64):
    Scratch tmp
    i32 n = render_base(&tmp, u64(value), 2, false)
    i64 wrote = 0
    if prefix:
        wrote = wrote + put_char('0', out)
        wrote = wrote + put_char('b', out)

    if digits > n:
        wrote = wrote + put_repeat('0', digits - n, out)

    return wrote + put_bytes(tmp[0..<i64(n)], out)

// ── the padded forms ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Each of these knows its body's width before writing a byte of it, so nothing
// here renders twice. That is why they are separate functions rather than a
// wrapper taking a putter: a wrapper would have to measure, and measuring means
// rendering into a counting sink and throwing it away.

// A `string` in a field.
i64 put_string_pad(string8 s, Pad p, Sink out):
    (i32 before, i32 after) = slack(i32(#len(s)), p)
    i64 wrote = put_repeat(p.fill, before, out)
    wrote = wrote + put_string(s, out)
    return wrote + put_repeat(p.fill, after, out)

// A signed decimal in a field.
i64 put_signed_pad($T value, Pad p, Sink out) where T is int && T in (i8, i16, i32, i64):
    Scratch tmp
    bool negative = value < 0
    u64 magnitude = u64(value)
    if negative:
        magnitude = 0 - u64(value)

    i32 n = render_base(&tmp, magnitude, 10, false)
    i32 body = n
    if negative:
        body = body + 1

    (i32 before, i32 after) = slack(body, p)
    i64 wrote = put_repeat(p.fill, before, out)
    if negative:
        wrote = wrote + put_char('-', out)

    wrote = wrote + put_bytes(tmp[0..<i64(n)], out)
    return wrote + put_repeat(p.fill, after, out)

// An unsigned decimal in a field.
i64 put_unsigned_pad($T value, Pad p, Sink out) where T is int && T in (u8, u16, u32, u64):
    Scratch tmp
    i32 n = render_base(&tmp, u64(value), 10, false)
    (i32 before, i32 after) = slack(n, p)
    i64 wrote = put_repeat(p.fill, before, out)
    wrote = wrote + put_bytes(tmp[0..<i64(n)], out)
    return wrote + put_repeat(p.fill, after, out)

// ── booleans ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// `true` or `false`, which is what the compiler's own renderer writes.
i64 put_bool(bool v, Sink out):
    if v:
        return put_string("true", out)

    return put_string("false", out)

// `v` as one of two words the caller chose — `yes`/`no`, `on`/`off`, `1`/`0`.
// The one opinion about a `bool` a program is likely to have, and the reason it
// is a parameter rather than three more functions.
i64 put_bool_as(bool v, string8 when_true, string8 when_false, Sink out):
    if v:
        return put_string(when_true, out)

    return put_string(when_false, out)

// ── text with the awkward bytes escaped ─────────────────────────────────────

// `s` between double quotes, with the eight escapes the language's own literals
// use and `\xHH` for every other byte below `0x20` or at `0x7F`.
//
// Bytes at or above `0x80` are written through untouched: they are part of a
// UTF-8 sequence this package cannot see the rest of, and escaping half of one
// produces text that says something different from what it was given.
i64 put_quoted(string8 s, Sink out):
    i64 wrote = put_char('"', out)
    i64 count = #len(s)
    i64 zero = 0
    for i in zero..<count:
        char8 b = s[i]
        if b == '"':
            wrote = wrote + put_string("\\\"", out)
        else if b == '\\':
            wrote = wrote + put_string("\\\\", out)
        else if b == '\n':
            wrote = wrote + put_string("\\n", out)
        else if b == '\r':
            wrote = wrote + put_string("\\r", out)
        else if b == '\t':
            wrote = wrote + put_string("\\t", out)
        else if b == '\0':
            wrote = wrote + put_string("\\0", out)
        else if b < ' ' || b == char8(0x7F):
            wrote = wrote + put_string("\\x", out)
            wrote = wrote + put_char(digit(u32(b) / 16, false), out)
            wrote = wrote + put_char(digit(u32(b) % 16, false), out)
        else:
            wrote = wrote + put_char(b, out)

    return wrote + put_char('"', out)

// ── floats ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// **Written in Dado rather than borrowed from C**, and the reason is the same one
// that keeps `<stdio.h>` out of this package: `%g` is a stream conversion and
// `snprintf` is not in C99 §4p6's freestanding set, so a float path built on it
// would decide the target for every importer.
//
// **`put_float` is not a shortest-round-trip conversion.** It writes the places
// the caller asked for, and a value that needs seventeen significant digits to
// name itself uniquely will not get them from a `decimals` of 6. Nor is it
// exact at many places: the fraction is scaled by one `f64` multiplication, so
// past about fifteen places the last digits are that product's and not the
// value's. The text that names a value exactly and reads back as it is
// `put_float_shortest`'s, below, which is a different deliverable with its own
// correctness argument (Ryū) and so has its own name rather than sitting
// silently underneath this one.

private const f64 SCALE_LIMIT = 1000000000000000000.0

// Whether `x` is a NaN, asked without `<math.h>`: a NaN is the one value that is
// not equal to itself, which is the definition rather than a trick.
bool is_nan($T x) where T is float:
    return x != x

// Whether `x` is an infinity, asked without `<math.h>`.
//
// **An infinity is the only non-zero value equal to twice itself.** Doubling a
// finite number either changes it or overflows to an infinity, which is then not
// equal to what it came from; doubling an infinity is that infinity. Zero is
// equal to twice itself too and is excluded first, and a NaN is equal to nothing
// including itself so it is excluded first as well.
//
// The obvious test — comparing against the largest finite `f64` — is what this
// replaced, and it was wrong in a way worth recording: the largest finite value
// has no literal in this language that does not itself round to it, and the
// spelling that was here (`1.0e308 * 10.0`) folds to an infinity, so the
// comparison read `inf > inf` and answered `false` for every infinity there is.
bool is_infinite($T x) where T is float:
    if is_nan(x):
        return false

    if x == 0.0:
        return false

    return x == x * 2.0

// `value` with exactly `decimals` places after the point, rounded half away from
// zero. `decimals` below 0 is read as 0, and above 17 as 17 — past that an `f64`
// has no more information to hand over and the digits would be an invention.
//
// `nan`, `inf` and `-inf` are written as those words. A magnitude at or above
// 10^18 is written in `d.ddde+NN` form, because the integer part no longer fits
// a `u64` and a fixed-point rendering of it would be wrong rather than long.
// **The template is one line over a concrete body**, and that shape is
// deliberate. Everything concrete in a package is emitted whether it is called
// or not, and a template a concrete function calls is instantiated along with
// it — so a template that *is* the implementation drags its whole
// instantiation into every importer the moment anything else in the package
// reaches it. Keeping the body concrete and the binder thin means `put_float`
// costs an importer one instantiation of one line, and the arithmetic below is
// emitted once regardless of how many widths call it.
i64 put_float($T value, i32 decimals, Sink out) where T is float:
    return put_f64(f64(value), decimals, out)

private i64 put_f64(f64 value, i32 decimals, Sink out):
    if value != value:
        return put_string("nan", out)

    if value != 0.0 && value == value * 2.0:
        if value < 0.0:
            return put_string("-inf", out)

        return put_string("inf", out)

    i32 places = decimals
    if places < 0:
        places = 0

    if places > 17:
        places = 17

    i64 wrote = 0
    f64 v = value
    if v < 0.0:
        wrote = wrote + put_char('-', out)
        v = 0.0 - v

    if v >= SCALE_LIMIT:
        return wrote + put_scientific(v, places, out)

    // The whole part and the fraction are taken apart **before** anything is
    // scaled. Scaling the value itself by `10^places` and splitting afterwards is
    // the obvious shape and it is wrong: `1e15` at 6 places is `1e21`, past a
    // `u64`, and an out-of-range float-to-integer conversion saturates — so the
    // integer came back as `2^64 - 1`, the rounding step wrapped it to 0, and the
    // answer read `0.000000`. Below `SCALE_LIMIT` the whole part always fits, and
    // the fraction is below 1, so `fraction * 10^places` is below `10^17` and
    // fits too, whatever the magnitude and whatever `places` is.
    //
    // `v - f64(integral)` is exact: a finite `f64` minus its own truncation is
    // representable, so the only rounding below is the one multiplication. The
    // fraction is then carried as an integer of `places` digits so the rounding
    // is one comparison on a whole number rather than a chain of float
    // operations whose error compounds with `places`.
    u64 factor = 1
    i32 zero = 0
    for _i in zero..<places:
        factor = factor * 10

    u64 integral = u64(v)
    f64 scaled = (v - f64(integral)) * f64(factor)
    u64 fraction = u64(scaled)
    f64 remainder = scaled - f64(fraction)
    if remainder >= 0.5:
        fraction = fraction + 1

    // Rounding the fraction up can carry into the whole part: `9.9999996` at 6
    // places is `10.000000`, not `9.1000000`.
    if fraction >= factor:
        fraction = fraction - factor
        integral = integral + 1

    Scratch whole_digits
    i32 wn = render_base(&whole_digits, integral, 10, false)
    wrote = wrote + put_bytes(whole_digits[0..<i64(wn)], out)
    if places == 0:
        return wrote

    wrote = wrote + put_char('.', out)
    Scratch tmp
    i32 n = render_base(&tmp, fraction, 10, false)
    if places > n:
        wrote = wrote + put_repeat('0', places - n, out)

    return wrote + put_bytes(tmp[0..<i64(n)], out)

// `d.ddde+NN`, for a magnitude a fixed-point rendering cannot hold. `v` is
// positive and finite here — both callers have already dealt with the sign and
// with the specials — so the loop terminates.
private i64 put_scientific(f64 v, i32 places, Sink out):
    i32 exponent = 0
    f64 m = v
    for m >= 10.0:
        m = m / 10.0
        exponent = exponent + 1

    i64 wrote = put_f64(m, places, out)
    wrote = wrote + put_char('e', out)
    wrote = wrote + put_char('+', out)
    Scratch tmp
    i32 n = render_base(&tmp, u64(exponent), 10, false)
    if n < 2:
        wrote = wrote + put_char('0', out)

    return wrote + put_bytes(tmp[0..<i64(n)], out)

// ── the shortest float that reads back ──────────────────────────────────────
//
// `put_float` writes the places it is asked for; this writes **the fewest
// significant digits that read back as the same value**, and of those the
// ones closest to it. `put_float_shortest(0.1, out)` is `0.1`, not
// `0.1000000000000000055511151231257827`, and `core:strings/parse`'s
// `to_f64` of what it writes is the value it was given, bit for bit, for every
// `f64` there is; the same holds for every `f32` through `to_f32`.
//
// **The digits are Ryū's.** `ryu_d2d` and `ryu_f2d` are ports of Ulf Adams'
// Ryū (`d2s.c`, `f2s.c`, the small-table configuration of
// `d2s_small_table.h` and its `umul128` fallback, for a language with no
// 128-bit integer), keeping upstream's names beside Dado's spellings so the two
// read side by side. Ryū is dual-licensed Apache-2.0 or Boost-1.0; this port
// takes it under the Boost Software License, whose notice follows.
//
//     Copyright 2018 Ulf Adams
//
//     Boost Software License - Version 1.0 - August 17th, 2003
//
//     Permission is hereby granted, free of charge, to any person or
//     organization obtaining a copy of the software and accompanying
//     documentation covered by this license (the "Software") to use,
//     reproduce, display, distribute, execute, and transmit the Software, and
//     to prepare derivative works of the Software, and to permit third-parties
//     to whom the Software is furnished to do so, all subject to the
//     following:
//
//     The copyright notices in the Software and this entire statement,
//     including the above license grant, this restriction and the following
//     disclaimer, must be included in all copies of the Software, in whole or
//     in part, and all derivative works of the Software, unless such copies or
//     derivative works are solely in the form of machine-executable object
//     code generated by a source language processor.
//
//     THE SOFTWARE IS PROVIDED "AS IS", WITHOUT WARRANTY OF ANY KIND, EXPRESS
//     OR IMPLIED, INCLUDING BUT NOT LIMITED TO THE WARRANTIES OF
//     MERCHANTABILITY, FITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE, TITLE AND
//     NON-INFRINGEMENT. IN NO EVENT SHALL THE COPYRIGHT HOLDERS OR ANYONE
//     DISTRIBUTING THE SOFTWARE BE LIABLE FOR ANY DAMAGES OR OTHER LIABILITY,
//     WHETHER IN CONTRACT, TORT OR OTHERWISE, ARISING FROM, OUT OF OR IN
//     CONNECTION WITH THE SOFTWARE OR THE USE OR OTHER DEALINGS IN THE
//     SOFTWARE.
//
// **The layout is Python's `repr`**, which is a fixed rule a reader can
// predict, and one every float reader accepts: a decimal point position `dp`
// (the value is `0.d1d2… × 10^dp`) from -3 to 16 is written positionally, with
// a `.0` when there is no fraction, and anything else as `d.ddd` with an
// exponent of at least two digits and its sign.
//
//     1.0   0.1   100.0   1234567890123456.0   0.0001   -0.0
//     1e+16   1e-05   1e+23   5e-324   1.7976931348623157e+308
//     inf   -inf   nan
//
// Every NaN is `nan`, whatever its sign bit, as `put_float` and `#render`
// write it. An `f32` is written from its own shortest digits, so
// `put_float_shortest(f32(0.1), out)` is `0.1` too, where widening it to
// `f64` first would write `0.10000000149011612`.
//
// **The template picks the width and nothing else**, the same one-line shape
// `put_float` has, so the arithmetic below is emitted once per width actually
// used.
i64 put_float_shortest($T value, Sink out) where T is float:
    if T == f32:
        return put_decimal(shortest32(f32(value)), out)

    return put_decimal(shortest64(f64(value)), out)

// `value` as `put_float_shortest` writes it, at the start of `into`. Answers
// how many bytes it wrote, or 0 and writes nothing when they do not fit; 24
// bytes always fit. It takes no `Sink`, so a script can call it.
//
//     [24]char8 buf
//     i32 n = fmt.render_float_shortest(0.1, buf[:])    // 3: buf starts "0.1"
#assert_raises
i32 render_float_shortest($T value, []char8 into) where T is float:
    Scratch text
    i32 n = 0
    if T == f32:
        n = decimal_text(&text, shortest32(f32(value)))
    else:
        n = decimal_text(&text, shortest64(f64(value)))

    return copy_into(&text, 0, n, into)

// A float as Ryū leaves it: `digits × 10^exp` with no trailing zero in
// `digits`, or one of the three values that have no digits.
private const i32 DEC_FINITE = 0
private const i32 DEC_ZERO = 1
private const i32 DEC_INF = 2
private const i32 DEC_NAN = 3

private type Decimal:
    i32  kind,
    bool neg,
    u64  digits,
    i32  exp,

private cunion Float64Bits: (f64 f, u64 u)
private cunion Float32Bits: (f32 f, u32 u)

private Decimal shortest64(f64 value):
    Float64Bits b = Float64Bits(value)
    u64 bits = b.u
    bool neg = bits >> 63 != 0
    u64 mantissa = bits & 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFF
    u32 exponent = u32((bits >> 52) & 0x7FF)
    if exponent == 0x7FF:
        if mantissa != 0:
            return (DEC_NAN, false, 0, 0)

        return (DEC_INF, neg, 0, 0)

    if exponent == 0 && mantissa == 0:
        return (DEC_ZERO, neg, 0, 0)

    (u64 digits, i32 exp) = ryu_small_int(mantissa, exponent)
    if digits == 0:
        (digits, exp) = ryu_d2d(mantissa, exponent)

    return (DEC_FINITE, neg, digits, exp)

private Decimal shortest32(f32 value):
    Float32Bits b = Float32Bits(value)
    u32 bits = b.u
    bool neg = bits >> 31 != 0
    u32 mantissa = bits & 0x7FFFFF
    u32 exponent = (bits >> 23) & 0xFF
    if exponent == 0xFF:
        if mantissa != 0:
            return (DEC_NAN, false, 0, 0)

        return (DEC_INF, neg, 0, 0)

    if exponent == 0 && mantissa == 0:
        return (DEC_ZERO, neg, 0, 0)

    (u32 digits, i32 exp) = ryu_f2d(mantissa, exponent)
    return (DEC_FINITE, neg, u64(digits), exp)

// Python's `repr` layout, into one buffer and out in one call.
private i64 put_decimal(Decimal d, Sink out):
    Scratch text
    i32 n = decimal_text(&text, d)
    return put_bytes(text[0..<i64(n)], out)

// `word` at the start of `text`, answering its length.
private i32 word_text(^Scratch text, string8 word):
    for i in 0..<#len(word):
        text^[i] = word[i]

    return i32(#len(word))

// `d` in Python's `repr` layout, at the start of `text`, answering how many
// bytes it wrote. The longest is 24 (`-2.2250738585072014e-308`); `Scratch`
// is 80.
private i32 decimal_text(^Scratch text, Decimal d):
    if d.kind == DEC_NAN:
        return word_text(text, "nan")

    if d.kind == DEC_INF:
        if d.neg:
            return word_text(text, "-inf")

        return word_text(text, "inf")

    if d.kind == DEC_ZERO:
        if d.neg:
            return word_text(text, "-0.0")

        return word_text(text, "0.0")

    // Ryū's digits carry no trailing zero except on its integer shortcut;
    // folding any into the exponent makes the layout below the only rule.
    u64 digits = d.digits
    i32 exp = d.exp
    for digits % 10 == 0:
        digits = digits / 10
        exp += 1

    Scratch ds
    i32 n = render_base(&ds, digits, 10, false)
    i32 dp = exp + n

    i32 at = 0
    if d.neg:
        text^[at] = '-'
        at += 1

    i32 zero = 0
    if dp > 0 - 4 && dp <= 16:
        if dp <= 0:
            text^[at] = '0'
            text^[at + 1] = '.'
            at += 2
            for _i in zero..<(0 - dp):
                text^[at] = '0'
                at += 1

            for i in zero..<n:
                text^[at] = ds[i]
                at += 1
        else if dp >= n:
            for i in zero..<n:
                text^[at] = ds[i]
                at += 1

            for _i in n..<dp:
                text^[at] = '0'
                at += 1

            text^[at] = '.'
            text^[at + 1] = '0'
            at += 2
        else:
            for i in zero..<dp:
                text^[at] = ds[i]
                at += 1

            text^[at] = '.'
            at += 1
            for i in dp..<n:
                text^[at] = ds[i]
                at += 1

        return at

    text^[at] = ds[0]
    at += 1
    if n > 1:
        text^[at] = '.'
        at += 1
        for i in 1..<n:
            text^[at] = ds[i]
            at += 1

    text^[at] = 'e'
    at += 1
    i32 e = dp - 1
    if e < 0:
        text^[at] = '-'
        e = 0 - e
    else:
        text^[at] = '+'

    at += 1
    if e < 10:
        text^[at] = '0'
        at += 1

    Scratch es
    i32 en = render_base(&es, u64(e), 10, false)
    for i in zero..<en:
        text^[at] = es[i]
        at += 1

    return at

// ── Ryū: the tables (`d2s_small_table.h`) ───────────────────────────────────

private const i32 POW5_INV_BITCOUNT = 125
private const i32 POW5_BITCOUNT = 125
private const i32 FLOAT_POW5_INV_BITCOUNT = 61
private const i32 FLOAT_POW5_BITCOUNT = 61
private const u32 POW5_TABLE_SIZE = 26

private [15][2]u64 POW5_INV_SPLIT2 = [
    [1, 2305843009213693952],
    [5955668970331000884, 1784059615882449851],
    [8982663654677661702, 1380349269358112757],
    [7286864317269821294, 2135987035920910082],
    [7005857020398200553, 1652639921975621497],
    [17965325103354776697, 1278668206209430417],
    [8928596168509315048, 1978643211784836272],
    [10075671573058298858, 1530901034580419511],
    [597001226353042382, 1184477304306571148],
    [1527430471115325346, 1832889850782397517],
    [12533209867169019542, 1418129833677084982],
    [5577825024675947042, 2194449627517475473],
    [11006974540203867551, 1697873161311732311],
    [10313493231639821582, 1313665730009899186],
    [12701016819766672773, 2032799256770390445],
]

private [22]u32 POW5_INV_OFFSETS = [
    0x54544554, 0x04055545, 0x10041000, 0x00400414, 0x40010000, 0x41155555,
    0x00000454, 0x00010044, 0x40000000, 0x44000041, 0x50454450, 0x55550054,
    0x51655554, 0x40004000, 0x01000001, 0x00010500, 0x51515411, 0x05555554,
    0x50411500, 0x40040000, 0x05040110, 0x00000000,
]

private [13][2]u64 POW5_SPLIT2 = [
    [0, 1152921504606846976],
    [0, 1490116119384765625],
    [1032610780636961552, 1925929944387235853],
    [7910200175544436838, 1244603055572228341],
    [16941905809032713930, 1608611746708759036],
    [13024893955298202172, 2079081953128979843],
    [6607496772837067824, 1343575221513417750],
    [17332926989895652603, 1736530273035216783],
    [13037379183483547984, 2244412773384604712],
    [1605989338741628675, 1450417759929778918],
    [9630225068416591280, 1874621017369538693],
    [665883850346957067, 1211445438634777304],
    [14931890668723713708, 1565756531257009982],
]

private [21]u32 POW5_OFFSETS = [
    0x00000000, 0x00000000, 0x00000000, 0x00000000, 0x40000000, 0x59695995,
    0x55545555, 0x56555515, 0x41150504, 0x40555410, 0x44555145, 0x44504540,
    0x45555550, 0x40004000, 0x96440440, 0x55565565, 0x54454045, 0x40154151,
    0x55559155, 0x51405555, 0x00000105,
]

private [26]u64 POW5_TABLE = [
    1, 5, 25, 125, 625, 3125, 15625, 78125, 390625,
    1953125, 9765625, 48828125, 244140625, 1220703125, 6103515625,
    30517578125, 152587890625, 762939453125, 3814697265625,
    19073486328125, 95367431640625, 476837158203125,
    2384185791015625, 11920928955078125, 59604644775390625,
    298023223876953125,
]

// ── Ryū: the arithmetic (`common.h`, `d2s_intrinsics.h`) ────────────────────

// `umul128`: the full product of two `u64`s, from four 32×32 products.
private (u64 hi, u64 lo) umul128(u64 a, u64 b):
    u64 a_lo = a & 0xFFFFFFFF
    u64 a_hi = a >> 32
    u64 b_lo = b & 0xFFFFFFFF
    u64 b_hi = b >> 32
    u64 b00 = a_lo * b_lo
    u64 b01 = a_lo * b_hi
    u64 b10 = a_hi * b_lo
    u64 b11 = a_hi * b_hi
    u64 mid1 = b10 + (b00 >> 32)
    u64 mid2 = b01 + (mid1 & 0xFFFFFFFF)
    u64 hi = b11 + (mid1 >> 32) + (mid2 >> 32)
    u64 lo = (mid2 << 32) | (b00 & 0xFFFFFFFF)
    return (hi, lo)

// `shiftright128`: bits `dist..<dist + 64` of `hi:lo`, `dist` in `1..<64`.
private u64 shiftright128(u64 lo, u64 hi, i32 dist):
    return (hi << (64 - dist)) | (lo >> dist)

private i32 pow5bits(i32 e):
    return i32(((u32(e) * 1217359) >> 19) + 1)

private u32 log10_pow2(i32 e):
    return (u32(e) * 78913) >> 18

private u32 log10_pow5(i32 e):
    return (u32(e) * 732923) >> 20

private u32 pow5_factor(u64 value):
    u64 v = value
    u32 count = 0
    for true:
        v = v * 14757395258967641293
        if v > 3689348814741910323:
            break

        count += 1

    return count

private bool multiple_of_pow5(u64 value, u32 p):
    return pow5_factor(value) >= p

private bool multiple_of_pow2(u64 value, u32 p):
    return value & ((u64(1) << p) - 1) == 0

// `double_computePow5`: `5^i` in the form Ryū's multiplications need.
private (u64 lo, u64 hi) compute_pow5(u32 i):
    u32 base = i / POW5_TABLE_SIZE
    u32 base2 = base * POW5_TABLE_SIZE
    u32 offset = i - base2
    u64 mul0 = POW5_SPLIT2[base][0]
    u64 mul1 = POW5_SPLIT2[base][1]
    if offset == 0:
        return (mul0, mul1)

    u64 m = POW5_TABLE[offset]
    (u64 high1, u64 low1) = umul128(m, mul1)
    (u64 high0, u64 low0) = umul128(m, mul0)
    u64 sum = high0 + low1
    if sum < high0:
        high1 += 1

    i32 delta = pow5bits(i32(i)) - pow5bits(i32(base2))
    u64 corr = u64((POW5_OFFSETS[i / 16] >> ((i % 16) << 1)) & 3)
    return (shiftright128(low0, sum, delta) + corr, shiftright128(sum, high1, delta))

// `double_computeInvPow5`: `5^-i` in the form Ryū's multiplications need.
private (u64 lo, u64 hi) compute_inv_pow5(u32 i):
    u32 base = (i + POW5_TABLE_SIZE - 1) / POW5_TABLE_SIZE
    u32 base2 = base * POW5_TABLE_SIZE
    u32 offset = base2 - i
    u64 mul0 = POW5_INV_SPLIT2[base][0]
    u64 mul1 = POW5_INV_SPLIT2[base][1]
    if offset == 0:
        return (mul0, mul1)

    u64 m = POW5_TABLE[offset]
    (u64 high1, u64 low1) = umul128(m, mul1)
    (u64 high0, u64 low0) = umul128(m, mul0 - 1)
    u64 sum = high0 + low1
    if sum < high0:
        high1 += 1

    i32 delta = pow5bits(i32(base2)) - pow5bits(i32(i))
    u64 corr = u64((POW5_INV_OFFSETS[i / 16] >> ((i % 16) << 1)) & 3)
    return (shiftright128(low0, sum, delta) + 1 + corr, shiftright128(sum, high1, delta))

// `mulShiftAll64`, the `umul128` variant: `(4m + 2, 4m, 4m - 1 - mm_shift)`
// times the power, each shifted right by `j`, as `(vr, vp, vm)`.
private (u64 vr, u64 vp, u64 vm) mul_shift_all64(u64 m_in, u64 mul0, u64 mul1, i32 j, u32 mm_shift):
    u64 m = m_in << 1
    (u64 tmp, u64 lo) = umul128(m, mul0)
    (u64 hi, u64 mid_lo) = umul128(m, mul1)
    u64 mid = tmp + mid_lo
    if mid < tmp:
        hi += 1

    u64 lo2 = lo + mul0
    u64 mid2 = mid + mul1
    if lo2 < lo:
        mid2 += 1

    u64 hi2 = hi
    if mid2 < mid:
        hi2 += 1

    u64 vp = shiftright128(mid2, hi2, j - 64 - 1)
    u64 vm = 0
    if mm_shift == 1:
        u64 lo3 = lo - mul0
        u64 mid3 = mid - mul1
        if lo3 > lo:
            mid3 -= 1

        u64 hi3 = hi
        if mid3 > mid:
            hi3 -= 1

        vm = shiftright128(mid3, hi3, j - 64 - 1)
    else:
        u64 lo3 = lo + lo
        u64 mid3 = mid + mid
        if lo3 < lo:
            mid3 += 1

        u64 hi3 = hi + hi
        if mid3 < mid:
            hi3 += 1

        u64 lo4 = lo3 - mul0
        u64 mid4 = mid3 - mul1
        if lo4 > lo3:
            mid4 -= 1

        u64 hi4 = hi3
        if mid4 > mid3:
            hi4 -= 1

        vm = shiftright128(mid4, hi4, j - 64)

    return (shiftright128(mid, hi, j - 64 - 1), vp, vm)

// ── Ryū: `f64` (`d2s.c`) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// `d2d_small_int`: an integer in `[1, 2^53)` is its own shortest digits.
// Answers 0 digits when the value is not one.
private (u64 digits, i32 exp) ryu_small_int(u64 ieee_mantissa, u32 ieee_exponent):
    u64 m2 = (u64(1) << 52) | ieee_mantissa
    i32 e2 = i32(ieee_exponent) - 1023 - 52
    if e2 > 0 || e2 < 0 - 52:
        return (0, 0)

    u64 mask = (u64(1) << (0 - e2)) - 1
    if m2 & mask != 0:
        return (0, 0)

    return (m2 >> (0 - e2), 0)

// `d2d`: the shortest `digits × 10^exp` inside the interval of decimals that
// round to the value, closest to it, ties to even.
private (u64 digits, i32 exp) ryu_d2d(u64 ieee_mantissa, u32 ieee_exponent):
    i32 e2 = 0
    u64 m2 = 0
    if ieee_exponent == 0:
        e2 = 1 - 1023 - 52 - 2
        m2 = ieee_mantissa
    else:
        e2 = i32(ieee_exponent) - 1023 - 52 - 2
        m2 = (u64(1) << 52) | ieee_mantissa

    bool accept_bounds = m2 & 1 == 0

    // Step 2: the interval of valid decimal representations.
    u64 mv = 4 * m2
    u32 mm_shift = 0
    if ieee_mantissa != 0 || ieee_exponent <= 1:
        mm_shift = 1

    // Step 3: into a decimal power base, with 128-bit arithmetic.
    u64 vr = 0
    u64 vp = 0
    u64 vm = 0
    i32 e10 = 0
    bool vm_is_trailing_zeros = false
    bool vr_is_trailing_zeros = false
    if e2 >= 0:
        u32 q = log10_pow2(e2)
        if e2 > 3:
            q -= 1

        e10 = i32(q)
        i32 k = POW5_INV_BITCOUNT + pow5bits(i32(q)) - 1
        i32 i = 0 - e2 + i32(q) + k
        (u64 p_lo, u64 p_hi) = compute_inv_pow5(q)
        (vr, vp, vm) = mul_shift_all64(m2, p_lo, p_hi, i, mm_shift)
        if q <= 21:
            // Only one of mp, mv and mm can be a multiple of 5, if any.
            if mv % 5 == 0:
                vr_is_trailing_zeros = multiple_of_pow5(mv, q)
            else if accept_bounds:
                vm_is_trailing_zeros = multiple_of_pow5(mv - 1 - u64(mm_shift), q)
            else if multiple_of_pow5(mv + 2, q):
                vp -= 1
    else:
        u32 q = log10_pow5(0 - e2)
        if 0 - e2 > 1:
            q -= 1

        e10 = i32(q) + e2
        i32 i = 0 - e2 - i32(q)
        i32 k = pow5bits(i) - POW5_BITCOUNT
        i32 j = i32(q) - k
        (u64 p_lo, u64 p_hi) = compute_pow5(u32(i))
        (vr, vp, vm) = mul_shift_all64(m2, p_lo, p_hi, j, mm_shift)
        if q <= 1:
            // mv = 4 * m2 always has at least two trailing 0 bits.
            vr_is_trailing_zeros = true
            if accept_bounds:
                vm_is_trailing_zeros = mm_shift == 1
            else:
                vp -= 1
        else if q < 63:
            vr_is_trailing_zeros = multiple_of_pow2(mv, q)

    // Step 4: the shortest decimal in the interval.
    i32 removed = 0
    u64 last_removed_digit = 0
    u64 output = 0
    if vm_is_trailing_zeros || vr_is_trailing_zeros:
        // The general case, which happens rarely (~0.7%).
        for vp / 10 > vm / 10:
            vm_is_trailing_zeros = vm_is_trailing_zeros && vm % 10 == 0
            vr_is_trailing_zeros = vr_is_trailing_zeros && last_removed_digit == 0
            last_removed_digit = vr % 10
            vr = vr / 10
            vp = vp / 10
            vm = vm / 10
            removed += 1

        if vm_is_trailing_zeros:
            for vm % 10 == 0:
                vr_is_trailing_zeros = vr_is_trailing_zeros && last_removed_digit == 0
                last_removed_digit = vr % 10
                vr = vr / 10
                vp = vp / 10
                vm = vm / 10
                removed += 1

        if vr_is_trailing_zeros && last_removed_digit == 5 && vr % 2 == 0:
            // Round even if the exact number is .....50..0.
            last_removed_digit = 4

        output = vr
        if (vr == vm && (!accept_bounds || !vm_is_trailing_zeros)) || last_removed_digit >= 5:
            output += 1
    else:
        // The common case (~99.3%).
        bool round_up = false
        if vp / 100 > vm / 100:
            round_up = vr % 100 >= 50
            vr = vr / 100
            vp = vp / 100
            vm = vm / 100
            removed += 2

        for vp / 10 > vm / 10:
            round_up = vr % 10 >= 5
            vr = vr / 10
            vp = vp / 10
            vm = vm / 10
            removed += 1

        output = vr
        if vr == vm || round_up:
            output += 1

    return (output, e10 + removed)

// ── Ryū: `f32` (`f2s.c`, over the `f64` tables) ─────────────────────────────

// `mulShift32`.
private u32 mul_shift32(u32 m, u64 factor, i32 shift):
    u64 factor_lo = factor & 0xFFFFFFFF
    u64 factor_hi = factor >> 32
    u64 bits0 = u64(m) * factor_lo
    u64 bits1 = u64(m) * factor_hi
    u64 sum = (bits0 >> 32) + bits1
    return u32(sum >> (shift - 32))

// `mulPow5InvDivPow2`: the table stores `[2^x / 5^y] + 1` in its low word, so
// the high word alone wants the `+ 1` back.
private u32 mul_pow5_inv_div_pow2(u32 m, u32 q, i32 j):
    u64 _, hi = compute_inv_pow5(q)
    return mul_shift32(m, hi + 1, j)

private u32 mul_pow5_div_pow2(u32 m, u32 i, i32 j):
    u64 _, hi = compute_pow5(i)
    return mul_shift32(m, hi, j)

private u32 pow5_factor32(u32 value):
    u32 v = value
    u32 count = 0
    for v % 5 == 0:
        v = v / 5
        count += 1

    return count

// `f2d`: `d2d` at single precision.
private (u32 digits, i32 exp) ryu_f2d(u32 ieee_mantissa, u32 ieee_exponent):
    i32 e2 = 0
    u32 m2 = 0
    if ieee_exponent == 0:
        e2 = 1 - 127 - 23 - 2
        m2 = ieee_mantissa
    else:
        e2 = i32(ieee_exponent) - 127 - 23 - 2
        m2 = (u32(1) << 23) | ieee_mantissa

    bool accept_bounds = m2 & 1 == 0

    // Step 2: the interval of valid decimal representations.
    u32 mv = 4 * m2
    u32 mp = 4 * m2 + 2
    u32 mm_shift = 0
    if ieee_mantissa != 0 || ieee_exponent <= 1:
        mm_shift = 1

    u32 mm = 4 * m2 - 1 - mm_shift

    // Step 3: into a decimal power base, with 64-bit arithmetic.
    u32 vr = 0
    u32 vp = 0
    u32 vm = 0
    i32 e10 = 0
    bool vm_is_trailing_zeros = false
    bool vr_is_trailing_zeros = false
    u32 last_removed_digit = 0
    if e2 >= 0:
        u32 q = log10_pow2(e2)
        e10 = i32(q)
        i32 k = FLOAT_POW5_INV_BITCOUNT + pow5bits(i32(q)) - 1
        i32 i = 0 - e2 + i32(q) + k
        vr = mul_pow5_inv_div_pow2(mv, q, i)
        vp = mul_pow5_inv_div_pow2(mp, q, i)
        vm = mul_pow5_inv_div_pow2(mm, q, i)
        if q != 0 && (vp - 1) / 10 <= vm / 10:
            // One removed digit is needed even when the loop below will not
            // run.
            i32 l = FLOAT_POW5_INV_BITCOUNT + pow5bits(i32(q - 1)) - 1
            last_removed_digit = mul_pow5_inv_div_pow2(mv, q - 1, 0 - e2 + i32(q) - 1 + l) % 10

        if q <= 9:
            // The largest power of 5 that fits in 24 bits is 5^10, but q <= 9
            // seems to be safe as well. Only one of mp, mv and mm can be a
            // multiple of 5, if any.
            if mv % 5 == 0:
                vr_is_trailing_zeros = pow5_factor32(mv) >= q
            else if accept_bounds:
                vm_is_trailing_zeros = pow5_factor32(mm) >= q
            else if pow5_factor32(mp) >= q:
                vp -= 1
    else:
        u32 q = log10_pow5(0 - e2)
        e10 = i32(q) + e2
        i32 i = 0 - e2 - i32(q)
        i32 k = pow5bits(i) - FLOAT_POW5_BITCOUNT
        i32 j = i32(q) - k
        vr = mul_pow5_div_pow2(mv, u32(i), j)
        vp = mul_pow5_div_pow2(mp, u32(i), j)
        vm = mul_pow5_div_pow2(mm, u32(i), j)
        if q != 0 && (vp - 1) / 10 <= vm / 10:
            j = i32(q) - 1 - (pow5bits(i + 1) - FLOAT_POW5_BITCOUNT)
            last_removed_digit = mul_pow5_div_pow2(mv, u32(i + 1), j) % 10

        if q <= 1:
            // mv = 4 * m2 always has at least two trailing 0 bits.
            vr_is_trailing_zeros = true
            if accept_bounds:
                vm_is_trailing_zeros = mm_shift == 1
            else:
                vp -= 1
        else if q < 31:
            vr_is_trailing_zeros = u64(mv) & ((u64(1) << (q - 1)) - 1) == 0

    // Step 4: the shortest decimal in the interval.
    i32 removed = 0
    u32 output = 0
    if vm_is_trailing_zeros || vr_is_trailing_zeros:
        // The general case, which happens rarely (~4.0%).
        for vp / 10 > vm / 10:
            vm_is_trailing_zeros = vm_is_trailing_zeros && vm % 10 == 0
            vr_is_trailing_zeros = vr_is_trailing_zeros && last_removed_digit == 0
            last_removed_digit = vr % 10
            vr = vr / 10
            vp = vp / 10
            vm = vm / 10
            removed += 1

        if vm_is_trailing_zeros:
            for vm % 10 == 0:
                vr_is_trailing_zeros = vr_is_trailing_zeros && last_removed_digit == 0
                last_removed_digit = vr % 10
                vr = vr / 10
                vp = vp / 10
                vm = vm / 10
                removed += 1

        if vr_is_trailing_zeros && last_removed_digit == 5 && vr % 2 == 0:
            // Round even if the exact number is .....50..0.
            last_removed_digit = 4

        output = vr
        if (vr == vm && (!accept_bounds || !vm_is_trailing_zeros)) || last_removed_digit >= 5:
            output += 1
    else:
        // The common case (~96.0%).
        for vp / 10 > vm / 10:
            last_removed_digit = vr % 10
            vr = vr / 10
            vp = vp / 10
            vm = vm / 10
            removed += 1

        output = vr
        if vr == vm || last_removed_digit >= 5:
            output += 1

    return (output, e10 + removed)

// ── the sinks this package can own ──────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Every sink here writes somewhere the caller already has. A sink over a stream
// belongs in `core:io`, for the header reason argued at the top of this file.

// ── a caller's own bytes ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// Where a `to_bytes` sink is up to. `into` is the caller's storage, `used` is
// how much of it holds bytes, and `overflowed` records that something was
// dropped — which a byte count alone cannot say, because a sink that filled its
// buffer exactly and one that was asked for more both answer `#len(into)`.
type Cursor:
    []char8 into,
    i64     used,
    bool    overflowed,

// A cursor over storage the caller owns.
Cursor over([]char8 into):
    return (into, 0, false)

// A sink that fills a cursor and drops what will not fit.
//
// **It truncates rather than failing**, and says so in `overflowed`. A formatter
// that stopped at the boundary would leave the caller a buffer whose contents
// depend on where the failure landed; one that reported and carried on leaves a
// prefix that is exactly what fits, which is what a log line or a status field
// wants. `written` is the prefix and `c.overflowed` is whether to trust it.
Sink to_bytes(^Cursor c):
    return (cursor_write, rawptr(c))

// What has landed in the cursor so far, as text. Free — a window over the
// caller's own storage, taken to the length that has been written.
string8 written(Cursor c):
    return string8(c.into[0..<c.used])

// Forget the bytes without forgetting the storage. The overflow flag goes with
// them, because it is a claim about the bytes.
void rewind(^Cursor c):
    c^.used = 0
    c^.overflowed = false
    return

private i64 cursor_write(rawptr data, rawptr bytes, i64 len):
    ^Cursor c = ^Cursor(data)
    if c == nil || bytes == nil || len <= 0:
        return 0

    i64 room = #len(c^.into) - c^.used
    i64 take = len
    if take > room:
        take = room
        c^.overflowed = true

    if take <= 0:
        return 0

    Raw src = Raw(bytes)
    []char8 from = src[0..<take]
    i64 at = c^.used
    i64 zero = 0
    for i in zero..<take:
        c^.into[at + i] = from[i]

    c^.used = at + take
    return take

// ── an owned, growable run ──────────────────────────────────────────────────

// A sink that appends to a run the caller owns, growing it as needed.
//
// **The run is taken by pointer**, and that is not a style choice: a by-value
// `ref` parameter is the callee's own copy of the handle, so a growth through
// one writes a new pointer, length and capacity into storage the caller never
// sees. ERR0813 refuses it and `^ref []char8` is the repair — here the caret is
// hidden behind the `rawptr` a sink carries, so the pointer is taken at the
// call to `to_run` and the procedure converts it back.
//
// Reserve before rendering if the size is roughly known; a sink cannot, because
// it learns the length one write at a time.
Sink to_run(^ref([]char8) dst):
    return (run_write, rawptr(dst))

private i64 run_write(rawptr data, rawptr bytes, i64 len):
    ^ref([]char8) r = ^ref([]char8)(data)
    if r == nil || bytes == nil || len <= 0:
        return 0

    Raw src = Raw(bytes)
    []char8 from = src[0..<len]
    i64 zero = 0
    for i in zero..<len:
        if !#append(r^, from[i]):
            return i

    return len

// ── measuring, and throwing away ────────────────────────────────────────────

// A sink that writes nothing and adds every byte it is offered to `n`.
//
// **This is how a length is found without a buffer**, which is the general
// answer the padded putters above avoid needing: render into one of these,
// read the count, allocate exactly, render again. The putters do not use it
// because each of them already knows its own width, and a formatter that
// rendered everything twice to align it would be paying for the general case
// at every call.
Sink counting(^i64 n):
    return (count_write, rawptr(n))

private i64 count_write(rawptr data, rawptr bytes, i64 len):
    ^i64 n = ^i64(data)
    if n == nil || len <= 0:
        return 0

    n^ = n^ + len
    return len

// A sink that takes everything and keeps none of it. Useful where a `Sink` is
// required and the bytes are not wanted — turning off a trace without changing
// the code that writes it.
Sink discard():
    return (discard_write, nil)

private i64 discard_write(rawptr data, rawptr bytes, i64 len):
    if len <= 0:
        return 0

    return len

// ── rendering a value of a type this package does not name ──────────────────

// `v`, in the form its type has, into `out`.
//
// One line, and the reason it is worth a name: the parameter is `$T`, so this
// function names no type at all and a caller renders a shape declared in its own
// package, with no import and nothing registered. It is the seam between the
// compiler's renderer and this package's destinations — the default form for
// anything, and every function above for the cases where the default is not what
// is wanted.
i64 put($T v, Sink out):
    return #render(v, out)

// ── print, println, write, writeln ──────────────────────────────────────────
//
// The four that take a message and put it somewhere, and the reason this
// package exists to hold them rather than the compiler: a message is a list of
// values of different types, one after another with nothing between them, and
// `..#any` is what lets a Dado function take one. Each element is rendered by
// `#render` in the form its own type has — the same renderer `#format` uses, so
// a value looks the same wherever it is written.
//
//     import "core:fmt"
//
//     fmt.println("n = ", count, " of ", total)
//
// **The destination is first on the two that take one**, which inverts this
// package's own sink-last rule. A pack must be last, so the position is decided
// by the grammar rather than chosen — and the inversion is safe here for the
// reason the rule was stated for: a `Sink` is named by its type, so a reader
// does not count arguments to find it.
//
// **They answer nothing.** Everything else in this package answers the count
// the sink took, because a short write is a real state a caller may want to
// compare against what it asked for. These four are the case where it is not:
// a message is written for a person to read, a caller that wanted the count
// wanted `put` and a sink of its own, and a `void` is what lets a call be a
// statement with no `_ =` in front of it. That is the whole of the difference,
// and it is why `write` here and `put_bytes` above are two functions rather
// than one.
//
// **And they do not fail.** A sink that takes nothing is already answered — a
// nil `proc` takes nothing and answers 0 — so there is no error a caller could
// act on and no `!` to write. A call site needs no `try`.
//
// ## Why the whole family is inside a `when`
//
// `#OS == #NONE` is a target with no C library, so there is no stdout to reach:
// `io.stdout_sink()` is `<stdio.h>`'s `stdout`, and `#render` of a float on a
// freestanding target is `ERR1004` because the conversion is libc's. Wrapping
// the family in `when #OS != #NONE:` means that on such a target the four names
// **do not exist**, and a call is refused with *package `fmt` declares no
// function `println`* — a refusal naming the target, rather than a link error
// or the silence a no-op would give. Every other line of this package stays
// callable there, which is what the wrapper is protecting.
//
// ## What this package now imports, and what that costs
//
// `import "core:io"` is the one import, and it is written at the top of the
// file because an `import` inside a `when` is ERR0201. That looks like it
// breaks the promise made at the head of this file — `<stdio.h>` reaching every
// importer — and it does not, because the emitter is driven by **liveness** and
// not by imports: measured on a `#NONE` target, a program that imports this
// package and calls `put_signed` emits `<stdint.h>` and `<stddef.h>` and
// nothing else. The four functions below are templates over a pack, so an
// importer that calls none of them instantiates none of them, reaches
// `io.stdout_sink` from nowhere, and pays for no header. The `import` itself is
// at the head of the file, beside `package`, because an `import` anywhere else
// is ERR0201 — which is also why it cannot be written inside this `when`.

when #OS != #NONE:
    // `args`, rendered one after another into `out`.
    //
    // The `for` is over a pack, so it is unrolled at the call: each copy has
    // its own `x` at that element's own type, and `#render` picks the form from
    // it. That is why one function serves every mixture of types a caller
    // writes, and why there is no format string to get wrong.
    void write(Sink out, ..#any args):
        for x in args:
            _ = #render(x, out)

    // `args`, and then a newline.
    void writeln(Sink out, ..#any args):
        for x in args:
            _ = #render(x, out)

        _ = put_char('\n', out)

    // `args`, on standard output.
    //
    // The sink is taken per call rather than held in a package variable. It is
    // two words built from a `foreign` constant, so there is nothing to cache
    // and nothing to initialise — and a program that redirected its own output
    // by other means gets the stream as it is at the call rather than as it was
    // at startup.
    void print(..#any args):
        write(io.stdout_sink(), ..args)

    // `args`, on standard output, and then a newline.
    void println(..#any args):
        writeln(io.stdout_sink(), ..args)
